  "categories": ["two-pointers", "monotonic-stack", "array"],
  "descriptionMd": "The array `height` describes a skyline of bars, each one unit wide, where `height[i]` is the height of the bar at position `i`.\n\nAfter a heavy rain, water collects in the dips between taller bars. Return the total number of units of water held by the skyline.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nheight = [0, 1, 0, 2, 1, 0, 1, 3, 2, 1, 2, 1]\n→ 6\n```\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nheight = [4, 2, 0, 3, 2, 5]\n→ 9\n```\nThe bars of height 4 and 5 enclose a basin: `2 + 4 + 1 + 2 = 9` units.\n\n**Follow-up:** can you solve it in O(n) time with O(1) extra space?",
  "constraints": ["1 ≤ height.length ≤ 2 · 10⁴", "0 ≤ height[i] ≤ 10⁵"],
  "hints": ["The water above a single position depends on the tallest bar to its left and the tallest bar to its right.", "Precomputing those maxima works in O(n) extra space. Can two pointers moving toward each other avoid the extra arrays?", "Whichever side currently has the lower maximum is the one whose water level is already decided."],
  "signature": {
    "functionName": "trap",
    "params": [{ "name": "height", "type": "int[]" }],
    "returns": "int"
  },
